<section xml:id="sec-ch03-05-TangentPlanes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Tangent Planes and Local Linearization</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Recall that given a function <m>w=F(x, y, z)</m>, we can intersect
      the function with the hyperplane <m>w=c</m> to get a 3-dimensional
      level surface <m>c=F(x, y, z)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, the sphere <m>x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=9</m> is a level surface
      of <m>w=x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}</m> when setting <m>w=9</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-05-sphere">
    <caption>The sphere of radius 3 centred at the origin.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-1. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-05-sphere" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-1.png">
          <shortdescription>The sphere of radius 3 centred at the origin.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The sphere of radius 3 centred at the origin.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      If a surface is given explicitly as <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, it can still be
      thought of as a level surface of a function <m>w=F(x, y, z)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Explicit and Level Surfaces</title>

    <p>
      The surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m> is a level surface of
      <m>w=z-f(x, y)</m>, by setting <m>w=0</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>f(x, y)=\frac{\sin x}{e^{xy}}</m>, find a function
        <m>w=F(x, y, z)</m> such that <m>f(x, y)</m> is one of its level
        surfaces.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By the remark above, the surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m> is a level
        surface of <m>w=z-f(x, y)</m>, obtained by setting <m>w=0</m>.
        Hence we may take
        <md>
          F(x, y, z) = z-\frac{\sin x}{e^{xy}}
        </md>
        and the graph of <m>f(x, y)</m> is exactly the level surface
        <m>F(x, y, z)=0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      We learned that a function <m>z=f(x, y)</m> is differentiable at
      <m>P(a, b, f(a, b))</m> if it looks like a plane when we zoom into
      the point <m>P</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    We will now give the equation that defines this plane and discuss where
    it comes from.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Tangent Plane to a Surface (Explicit)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, the tangent plane
        to the surface at the point ( <m>a, b, f(a, b)</m> ) is given by
        the equation
        <md>
          z=f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b)
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find an equation for the tangent plane to the surface
        <m>1-0.5y^2-0.5x^2</m> at the point
        <m>\left(\sqrt2/2, \sqrt2/2, 0.5\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>f(x, y)=1-0.5y^2-0.5x^2</m> and
        <m>(a, b)=\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m>.
        The partial derivatives are <m>f_x = -x</m> and <m>f_y = -y</m>, so
        <md>
          f_x\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \quad f_y\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
        </md>
        and
        <m>f\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = 1-0.5\cdot\frac{1}{2}-0.5\cdot\frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{2}</m>,
        which agrees with the given point. Substituting into the formula
        for the tangent plane:
        <md>
          z=f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b) = \frac{1}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\left(x-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\left(y-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)
        </md>.
      </p>
      <!--Since $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}=\frac{1}{2}$, this simplifies to
      \begin{gather*}
      z = \frac{1}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}x+\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}y+\frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}(x+y)
      \end{gather*}-->
    </solution>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-05-paraboloid-tangent-plane">
    <caption>The paraboloid z equals 1 minus x squared over 2 minus y squared over 2, with its tangent plane at the point (a, b, f(a,b)).</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-2. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-05-paraboloid-tangent-plane" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-2.png">
          <shortdescription>The paraboloid z equals 1 minus x squared over 2 minus y squared over 2, with its tangent plane at the point (a, b, f(a,b)).</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The paraboloid <m>z=1-\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{y^2}{2}</m>, with its tangent plane at the point <m>(a, b, f(a,b))</m>.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <m>f(x, y)= 1-0.5y^2-0.5x^2</m> in green/blue and the tangent plane in
    grey
  </p>

  <note>
    <p>
      The equation for the tangent plane depends on the point
      <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <note>
    <p>
      Remember that every surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m> is a level surface of
      some function <m>w=F(x, y, z)</m>. Hence, to see where the equation
      for the tangent plane comes from, we work with (implicit) level
      surfaces of the form <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m>, where <m>d</m> is a scalar
      (not a variable).
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      If <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is a 3D-curve on the surface <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m>
      passing through the point ( <m>a, b, c</m> ) on the surface at time
      <m>t=t_{0}</m>, then
      <md>
        \nabla F(a, b, c) \cdot \vec{r}^{\prime}\left(t_{0}\right)=0
      </md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is true for <alert>EVERY</alert> differentiable curve on the
      surface passing through ( <m>a, b, c</m> ).
    </p>
  </remark>

