  <p>
    The graphs above show only the plots; you can drag and zoom them. To see the expressions behind them, or to save a copy and create your own version of the graphs, open them in a new page from here:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Gradient and Tangent Plane</title>

    <p>
      Given a differentiable (level) surface <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m>, the
      tangent plane to the surface at the point ( <m>a, b, c</m> ) is the
      plane whose normal vector, given by <m>\nabla F(a, b, c)</m>, is
      orthogonal to the tangent vectors of every (differentiable) curve on
      the surface passing through ( <m>a, b, c</m> ).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    This observation gives rise to the following formula:
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Tangent Plane to a Surface (Implicit)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable surface <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m> and a point
        <m>(a, b, c)</m> on the surface, the tangent plane to the surface at
        the point <m>(a, b, c)</m> has normal vector <m>\nabla F(a, b, c)</m>
        and is given by the equation
        <md>
          F_{x}(a, b, c)(x-a)+F_{y}(a, b, c)(y-b)+F_{z}(a, b, c)(z-c)=0
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find an equation for the tangent plane to the surface
        <m>x+ y^2 + z^3 = 1</m> at the point <m>\left( 0,0,1\right)</m>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-05-ex-11-3">
        <caption>The surface x plus y squared plus z cubed equals 1, and its tangent plane at the point (0,0,1).</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-5. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-05-ex-11-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_20_89e41e7bb5c056e57e38g-5.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface x plus y squared plus z cubed equals 1, and its tangent plane at the point (0,0,1).</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface <m>x+y^2+z^3=1</m>, and its tangent plane at the point <m>(0,0,1)</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <m>x+ y^2 + z^3 = 1</m> in green and the tangent plane in light blue
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The surface is the level surface <m>F(x, y, z)=0</m> of the
        function <m>F(x, y, z)=x+y^{2}+z^{3}-1</m>, and the point
        <m>(0,0,1)</m> is indeed on the surface since <m>0+0^2+1^3=1</m>.
        The partial derivatives are <m>F_x = 1</m>, <m>F_y = 2y</m>, and
        <m>F_z = 3z^2</m>, so the normal vector to the tangent plane is
        <md>
          \nabla F(0,0,1) = \left\langle 1, 2(0), 3(1)^2\right\rangle = \langle 1, 0, 3\rangle
        </md>
        Substituting into the formula for the (implicit) tangent plane:
        <md>
          1(x-0)+0(y-0)+3(z-1)=0 \Rightarrow x+3(z-1)=0
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Approximations</title>

    <p>
      As we saw in <xref ref="example-ch03-05-ex-11-3"/>, the equation
      <m>x+ y^2 + z^3 = 1</m> is more <q>complicated</q> than the equation
      for the tangent plane <m>x+3(z-1)</m> at <m>\left( 0,0,1\right)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is important because we can use the tangent plane
      <m>x+3(z-1)</m> to approximate <m>x+ y^2 + z^3 = 1</m> for points
      <q>close</q> to <m>(0,0,1)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Tangent Planes and Approximations</title>

    <p>
      Given a differentiable surface <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m>, its tangent plane
      at the point ( <m>a, b, c</m> ) can be used to approximate the
      surface for points close to ( <m>a, b, c</m> ).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the surface <m>f(x, y)=xye^{xy} </m>, use tangent planes to
        approximate <m>f(0.1,0.1)</m> and <m>f(0.1, 1.1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The idea is to choose, for each input, a nearby input <m>(a, b)</m>
        at which <m>f</m> and its partial derivatives are easy to evaluate,
        and then approximate <m>f</m> by its tangent plane at <m>(a, b)</m>.
        By the product and chain rules, the partial derivatives of
        <m>f(x, y)=xye^{xy}</m> are
        <md>
          f_x = ye^{xy}+xye^{xy}\cdot y = ye^{xy}(1+xy), \quad f_y = xe^{xy}+xye^{xy}\cdot x = xe^{xy}(1+xy)
        </md>
        For <m>f(0.1,0.1)</m> the closest convenient input is
        <m>(a, b)=(0,0)</m>. There <m>f(0,0)=0</m>, <m>f_x(0,0)=0</m>, and
        <m>f_y(0,0)=0</m>, so the tangent plane is
        <md>
          z = 0+0(x-0)+0(y-0) = 0
        </md>
        and hence <m>f(0.1,0.1)\approx 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        For <m>f(0.1,1.1)</m> the closest convenient input is
        <m>(a, b)=(0,1)</m>. There <m>f(0,1)=0\cdot 1\cdot e^{0}=0</m>,
        <m>f_x(0,1)=1\cdot e^{0}(1+0)=1</m>, and
        <m>f_y(0,1)=0\cdot e^{0}(1+0)=0</m>, so the tangent plane is
        <md>
          z = 0+1(x-0)+0(y-1) = x
        </md>
        and hence <m>f(0.1,1.1)\approx 0.1</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all points on the surface <m>z=x^{2}-2 x y-y^{2}- x+ y</m>
        where the tangent plane is horizontal (parallel to the plane
        <m>z=0</m> ).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Writing the surface as <m>z=f(x, y)</m> with
        <m>f(x, y)=x^{2}-2 x y-y^{2}- x+ y</m>, the tangent plane at the
        point <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m> is
        <md>
          z=f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b)
        </md>
        which is horizontal exactly when <m>f_x(a, b)=0</m> and
        <m>f_y(a, b)=0</m>, since then the equation reduces to the constant
        plane <m>z=f(a, b)</m>. The partial derivatives are
        <md>
          f_x = 2x-2y-1, \quad f_y = -2x-2y+1
        </md>
        Setting both equal to zero (<m>2x-2y-1=0</m> and
        <m>-2x-2y+1=0</m>) and adding the two equations gives
        <md>
          (2x-2y-1)+(-2x-2y+1) = 0 \Rightarrow -4y = 0 \Rightarrow y=0
        </md>
        Substituting <m>y=0</m> into <m>f_x=0</m> gives
        <m>2x-1=0 \Rightarrow x=\frac{1}{2}</m>, and this also satisfies
        <m>f_y = -2\cdot\frac{1}{2}-0+1 = 0</m>. Finally
        <md>
          f\left(\frac{1}{2}, 0\right) = \frac{1}{4}-0-0-\frac{1}{2}+0 = -\frac{1}{4}
        </md>
        So the only point where the tangent plane is horizontal is
        <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, 0, -\frac{1}{4}\right)</m>, where the tangent
        plane is <m>z=-\frac{1}{4}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>3D gradient <m>\nabla F(x, y, z)</m> vs 2D gradient <m>\nabla f(x, y)</m></title>

    <p>
      Do not confuse the 3D gradient <m>\nabla F(x, y, z)</m> with the 2D
      gradient <m>\nabla f(x, y)</m> we saw when studying directional
      derivatives. Here we summarize their different applications:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given an (explicit) 3-dimensional surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, its
            2 -dimensional gradient
            <m>\nabla f(a, b)=\left\langle f_{x}(a, b), f_{y}(a, b)\right\rangle</m>
            tells you the direction (in the <m>x y</m> plane) of steepest
            ascent at the input <m>( a, b )</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Given an (implicit) 3-dimensional surface <m>d=F(x, y, z)</m>,
            its 3-dimensional gradient
            <m>\nabla F(a, b, c)=\left\langle F_{x}(a, b, c), F_{y}(a, b, c), F_{z}(a, b, c)\right\rangle</m>
            gives you a normal vector to the tangent plane to the surface at
            the point <m>(a, b, c)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Given a differentiable function <m>f(x, y)</m>, we saw that we can
      approximate this function for inputs close to <m>(a, b)</m> by using
      the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at the point ( <m>a, b, f(a, b)</m> ).
      That is,
      <md>
        f(x, y) \approx f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b)
      </md>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    Since the expression on the right only involves constants and linear
    powers of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, this approximation is referred to as
    the linearization of <m>f(x, y)</m> at the input  <m>(a, b)</m> .
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Linearization of a Function at a Point</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable function <m>f(x, y)</m>, its linearization
        (or linear approximation) at the input ( <m>a, b</m> ) is given by
        <md>
          L(x, y)=f(a, b)+f_{x}(a, b)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b)(y-b)
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      Since the linearization of <m>f</m> at an input ( <m>a, b</m> ) is
      just the equation of the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at that input, the
      main application is the same: Approximate <m>f</m> with a
      <q>simpler</q> function.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the linearization of <m>f(x, y)=x^{3}-2x y+ y^2- y</m> at the
        input <m>(1,2)</m>. Use this to approximate <m>f(0.9,2.1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first compute the value of <m>f</m> and of its partial
        derivatives at the input <m>(1,2)</m>:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f(1,2) = 1^3 - 2(1)(2) + 2^2 - 2 = 1 - 4 + 4 - 2 = -1</mrow>
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) = 3x^2 - 2y \Rightarrow f_x(1,2) = 3(1)^2 - 2(2) = 3 - 4 = -1</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) = -2x + 2y - 1 \Rightarrow f_y(1,2) = -2(1) + 2(2) - 1 = -2 + 4 - 1 = 1</mrow>
        </md>
        Substituting these into the formula for the linearization at
        <m>(1,2)</m>:
        <md>
          L(x, y) = f(1,2) + f_x(1,2)(x-1) + f_y(1,2)(y-2) = -1 - (x-1) + (y-2)
        </md>
        Since the input <m>(0.9, 2.1)</m> is close to <m>(1,2)</m>, we can
        approximate <m>f(0.9,2.1)</m> by the linearization:
        <md>
          f(0.9, 2.1) \approx L(0.9, 2.1) = -1-(0.9-1) + (2.1-2) = -1+0.1+0.1 = -0.8
        </md>
        For comparison, the exact value is
        <m>f(0.9,2.1) = (0.9)^3 - 2(0.9)(2.1) + (2.1)^2 - 2.1 = -0.741</m>,
        so the approximation is fairly close.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When talking about partial derivatives, we saw the follow formulas:
      <md>
        f(a+\Delta x, b)-f(a, b) \approx f_{x}(a, b) \Delta x
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        f(a, b+\Delta y)-f(a, b) \approx f_{y}(a, b) \Delta y
      </md>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we want to approximate a change in a function <m>f(x, y)</m> in
      both coordinates, we have a similar formula.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Approximating Change</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a differentiable function <m>f(x, y)</m>, we can approximate
        small changes in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> using
        <md>
          f(a+\Delta x, b+\Delta y)-f(a, b) \approx f_{x}(a, b) \Delta x+f_{y}(a, b) \Delta y
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes, this equation is written using the total differential of
        <m>f</m>, as
        <md>
          d f=f_{x}(a, b) d x+f_{y}(a, b) d y
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-05-ex-11-7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The volume of a right circular cone with radius <m>r</m> and height
        <m>h</m> is <m>V=\pi r^{2} h / 3</m>. Approximate the change in the
        volume of the cone when the radius changes from <m>r=6</m> to
        <m>r=6.1</m> and the height changes from <m>h=4</m> to <m>h=3.9</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>V(r,h) = \frac{\pi r^2 h}{3}</m>, the radius changes from
        <m>r=6</m> by <m>\Delta r = 0.1</m> and the height changes from
        <m>h=4</m> by <m>\Delta h = -0.1</m>. We compute the partial
        derivatives of <m>V</m> at the input <m>(6,4)</m>:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>V_r(r,h) = \frac{2\pi r h}{3} \Rightarrow V_r(6,4) = \frac{2\pi (6)(4)}{3} = 16\pi</mrow>
          <mrow>V_h(r,h) = \frac{\pi r^2}{3} \Rightarrow V_h(6,4) = \frac{\pi (6)^2}{3} = 12\pi</mrow>
        </md>
        By the approximating-change formula:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>V(6.1, 3.9) - V(6,4) \approx V_r(6,4) \Delta r + V_h(6,4) \Delta h = 16\pi (0.1) + 12\pi (-0.1)</mrow>
          <mrow>= 1.6\pi - 1.2\pi = 0.4\pi \approx 1.26</mrow>
        </md>
        So the volume increases by approximately <m>0.4\pi \approx 1.26</m>
        cubic units. Even though the height decreases, the change coming
        from the radius is larger, so the net change in the volume is
        positive.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>This formula is useful when:</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            You do not have the explicit function, but you do know its
            rates of change.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The original function is too convoluted to work with, this
            approach can be more computationally efficient.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    The formula for the linearization of a function generalizes easily to
    functions of more than two inputs.
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Linearization in Higher Dimensions</title>

    <p>
      Given a differentiable function <m>w=f(x, y, z)</m>, its
      linearization at the input ( <m>a, b, c</m> ) is given by
      <md>
        L(x, y, z)=f(a, b, c)+f_{x}(a, b, c)(x-a)+f_{y}(a, b, c)(y-b)+f_{z}(a, b, c)(z-c)
      </md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

</section>
